bootrom — auditable reverse-engineering of Novatek mask-ROMs
=============================================================

This tree reconstructs the on-chip mask-ROM of each supported die into
reviewable C and ARM assembly, mirroring OpenIPC/openhisilicon's bootrom/.
The ROM is what runs before any flash code: it picks a boot source from the
strap pins, falls back from a missing loader through SD, Ethernet, USB and
UART, checks the loader it finds and jumps to it. Knowing exactly what it
does is what makes a bricked camera recoverable.

Per-die layout:

  bootrom/<die>/
    MANIFEST                  tracked: SoCs, run and home address, dump
                              size, sha256, first 16 bytes, how it was dumped
    Makefile                  disasm | build | verify | clean
    .expected-symbols         the functions re.elf must define (CI gate)
    re/                       clean-room reverse-engineered source
      <die>-regmap.h          umbrella header
      regmap-*.h              per-block register offsets, only what's used
      start.S                 reset vectors, CPU setup, stack, the
                              exported-routine table the loaders call
      *.c                     C bodies, one file per subsystem
      re.lds                  links at the address the ROM runs at
    scratchpad/               human-readable analysis notes
      bootrom.txt             hex + mnemonic + commentary (vector page)
      functions.txt           function ledger: address, name, signature
      sram.txt                SRAM and global data the ROM uses
      regmap.txt              every MMIO register the ROM touches
      split.txt               which ledger functions live in which file

The raw .bin and the disassembler output stay local — see .gitignore. Each
contributor reproduces them with scripts/dump.sh and scripts/disasm.sh.

Supported dies
--------------

  na51089   NT98560, NT98561, NT98562, NT98566   32 KiB, runs at 0,
                                                 home 0x80000000

Workflow
--------

  # 1. Dump the mask-ROM from a live camera: root SSH and busybox devmem.
  #    It is read a word at a time from its home address.
  bootrom/scripts/dump.sh <camera> na51089

  # 2. Verify the local dump matches the tracked MANIFEST.
  bootrom/scripts/verify.sh na51089

  # 3. Produce a disassembly (local-only) at the address the ROM runs at.
  bootrom/scripts/disasm.sh na51089

  # 4. Sanity-build the reconstructed source; it must compile cleanly under
  #    arm-none-eabi-gcc with -nostdlib -ffreestanding.
  make -C bootrom/na51089 build

The build target produces re.elf. It is NOT a byte-identical
reconstruction: matching the original image would need Novatek's exact
compiler and flags. It is a well-formedness gate, and a record of the
behaviour, function by function, with the addresses in the original.

How na51089 was checked
-----------------------

re.elf links to exactly 32 KiB with the exported table at 0x7FB8, as in the
ROM. Beyond the compile gate:

  start.S     linked with the C entry points at their original addresses,
              its 0x320 bytes are identical to the ROM's 0x0000..0x031f
  export.S    the same, for the 72-byte table at 0x7FB8..0x7FFF
  lib.c       run side by side with the ROM's own code under qemu-arm on
  loader.c    random inputs (20k rounds per helper; 11k random register
              scripts for hdr_script_run, every op and branch): identical
  lzma.c      byte-identical output on seven LZMA streams (several lc/lp/pb
  lz.c        settings), and LZ_Uncompress on a 40 KB stream
  usb.c       the USB descriptors and the SCSI replies are byte-identical
  usb_msc.c   to the ROM's const data
  net.c       the frame templates are byte-identical; the console letters
  eth.c       match a serial capture of the Ethernet boot on an NT98562

Everything else is checked by reading against the disassembly only. Each
function carries its original address, and faithful quirks (including
outright ROM bugs, such as memcpy8 dropping words at some alignments) are
kept and commented. TODO(headers) marks a constant defined locally that
belongs in a shared header.

What the ROM does (na51089)
---------------------------

The NT9856x ROM code Application Note describes the boot source table and
the letters the ROM prints on UART0 (115200 8N1); the reconstruction
confirms and extends it:

  N           ROM start
  P D R E     SPI NOR, SPI NAND (on-die ECC), SPI NAND (RS ECC), eMMC
  S1          SD card
  H E/O/I     Ethernet, built-in PHY / RMII out / RMII in
  U           USB
  K2..K9      failures; K9 is a loader that fails the checksum
  X A R S     secure boot: enabled, AES, RSA, encrypted data area
  T           jump to the loader

With no valid loader in flash it goes flash, SD, Ethernet, USB, UART. Once a
DHCP server answers, the Ethernet stage waits for loader.bin over TFTP with
no timeout, so on a network it is in practice the last source. The UART
stage takes the write loader in 16-byte commands. re/ has the details.

Running it under emulation
--------------------------

qemu-bench's NA51089 machines model this ROM's behaviour in C
(nvt-bootrom.c), written from this reconstruction; defib recovers a blank
emulated board through it over UART and over Ethernet. Running re.elf
itself there, as openhisilicon's av300 ROM runs in qemu-hisilicon, is
future work.
